WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.500
"The Large Bathers" by Paul Cezanne

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.040
is a painting

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.400
that dawns on you.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.160
At first you see the whole:

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.400
a group of women bathing by a river,

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.540
a subject that's anything but original.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.020
The canvas is composed in a balanced geometry of

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.240
triangles: one for the group on the left,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.800
one for those on the right,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.720
and one comprising them all,

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.520
bounded by the leaning trees.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.660
But after a minute, you start to notice things

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.700
like the figures behind the river,

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.920
or the swimmer in the water

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.540
or, almost disturbingly, that none of the bathers

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.680
have any definable faces, or

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.440
that their bodies seem to fold into one another,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:51.900
as if they've been painted over -

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.740
but not completely.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.320
The more you stare at it,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.940
the more the canvas seems

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.680
a chaos of elements

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.140
deliriously rendered

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:03.360
with a spontaneity that feels almost

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.060
negligent.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.680
Of course, we know that Cezanne

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.220
labored on this painting for seven years,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.200
all the way up to his death.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.200
Spontaneity is certainly part of the message.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.480
But the painter was intensely purposeful in

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.320
what he wanted to say, and

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.080
how he wanted to convey it.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.120
From our perspective,

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.180
across the gulf of cubism and

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.920
all imaginable forms of abstraction,

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.160
it's hard for Cezanne's distortions of

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.360
color and form to shock us -

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.340
yet, that's exactly what they did in 1906,

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.520
at the painting's first exhibition.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.520
It's not wrong to say that Cezanne initiated

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.540
what became the 20th century's

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.300
endless experimentation.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.400
As Picasso himself said,

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.120
"Cezanne is the father of us all."

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.760
In this way, he can sort of be seen as

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.320
the bridge between what came before

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.120
and all that was to come.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.500
"The Large Bathers" reaches in the past,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.200
recalling similar scenes of nudes or bathers

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.540
like those by Titian,

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:00.740
or Nicolas Poussin,

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.700
or Thomas Eakins, among others.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.080
Certain of the figures evoke

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.980
postures from older works:

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.980
the woman on the left, striding upwards

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.340
with the tree, for example,

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.980
bears a resemblance to an 18th-century sculpture

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.980
of the goddess Diana.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.980
Or, take the woman on the right, here,

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.980
who's raised knee and shoulders

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.980
remember the classical Venus de Milo

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.940
by Alexandros of Antioch.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.660
And, perhaps it should be noted that

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.040
none of the 14 women we see here

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.380
were drawn from real models.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.140
Cezanne took his examples

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.480
from other works and picture books,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.740
adding to that disquieting sense of remove

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.340
that these figures have from any

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.060
identifiable personalities.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.920
That remove - that alienation - is

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.000
echoed everywhere in the painting.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.500
Of the 14 women, six are simply

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.240
turned away from the viewer,

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.580
a few are even in the process

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.340
of dissolving or emerging

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.200
from the foliage all around them.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.040
Most unsettling of all, the figures

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.200
are pocked with areas of white,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.320
which, on closer examination,

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.840
proves not to be any paint at all,

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.080
but the bare canvas itself -

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:06.560
interrupting the scene as if

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.140
some force from a world beyond.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.940
What is Cezanne trying to do here?

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.580
What do these disturbances amount to?

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.420
What are the effects of his short, flat brushstrokes,

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.560
of distilling everything into its

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.280
geometrical basis, or

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.240
freezing in paint that which

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.900
doesn't appear to be a scene at all,

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:27.140
but something on the verge

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.800
of becoming a scene -

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.340
or else, of dissolving out of one.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:34.140
Cezanne is emerging out of Impressionism,

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.260
a movement that was obsessed

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:37.880
with how colors and light

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.600
really appeared to the eye.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.160
How did the atmosphere -

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.860
the rain or the sunset -

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.560
affect the visual impression of the observer?

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.560
The Impressionists explored this by

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.200
going out into nature and

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.300
painting rapidly, applying

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.500
just what they saw

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.060
to the canvas in front of them.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.400
Cezanne took the palette of the Impressionists,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.480
retaining the brilliant, often surprising colors

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.560
used to evoke nature's effect on us;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.020
but he took color a step further

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.100
and applied it to express form as well.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.560
Ever since the Renaissance,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.480
depth was rendered via perspective.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.480
Cezanne seems to realize that

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.560
Renaissance perspective is only a kind of

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.920
code, by which we understand

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:20.900
the third dimension in a canvas.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.180
Seeing itself,

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.160
the perception as distinct from the understanding,

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.160
may not work on those principles;

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:30.220
it may be far more unruly than that.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.260
This is why he didn't shy away from

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.400
distortion of objects, of using

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:35.920
colors to bring elements in the

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.440
background forward, or

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.840
melt things in the foreground

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.820
into the architecture of colors

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:41.720
that hold them there.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.280
There's a difference between

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.720
the way we know what we see

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.420
and how we actually see it.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.600
Our minds are so incredibly good

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.840
at taking the anarchic varieties

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.840
of sensible experience

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.480
and within milliseconds, composing them

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:58.840
into intelligible wholes.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.840
It happens so fast that we can't even

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.040
access that actual moment of perception,

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.540
and for the most part, we should be

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.840
happy about that, because

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.200
raw experience, like a kind of trauma,

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.160
is just too much for us to withstand.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.160
In "The Large Bathers," Cezanne uses

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:17.160
his various techniques to visit

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.160
that moment of perception.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.160
Confronted with it aesthetically,

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.160
all that's unsettling about experience

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.180
is frozen for our contemplation.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.180
In life, it's a bridge we just can't cross -

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:29.920
take a look at that man

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:31.760
on the other side of the river.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.080
Many think that this figure is

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.320
Cezanne himself, situated between

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.680
the classical, constructed diorama of bathers

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:41.740
and the pastoral, impressionistic church scene

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:42.900
in the distance.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.580
I'd bet, if we could turn the painting around

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:47.700
and see it from that perspective,

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.120
it would look just as sensual, erotic and beautiful

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:52.120
as something by Titian or

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.720
any other painter who depicted

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.960
a similar scene in the past.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.580
But from our vantage,

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.520
the one Cezanne gave to us,

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.900
we're allowed a fugitive access

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:06.780
to something far more disturbing:

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:08.600
the real.

